Introduction To The Comparative Theories
Realism and liberalism are the two principal comparative theories used to understand and analyse
global politics. These frameworks offer fundamentally contrasting perspectives on the behaviour of
states, the nature of international relations, and the potential for cooperation or conflict in a
globalised world. While realism centres on power politics, the inevitability of conflict, and a
pessimistic view of human nature, liberalism highlights the potential for cooperation,
interdependence, and the role of international organisations in promoting global governance.
These theories are important as they are the main focus of the compulsory 12-marker question. In
this 12-marker, you also need to draw a synoptic link to the thinkers/ideas you studied in the core
political ideas part of the specification:
● Conservatism, with its pessimistic view of human nature, often aligns with realism. This is
particularly true of Thomas Hobbes, who argues that without an overarching state authority
to impose order, there will be anarchy.
○ Realists believe that as there is no overarching authority in global politics, there is
internatonal anarchy.
● Liberalism is naturally aligned with the views of liberalism in global politics. This is because
it is optimistic about collective progress and believes that people can be self-interested
