The Security Dilemna
● Realists argue that international anarchy inevitably creates a security dilemna.
● In an anarchic system, when one state takes measures to increase its security—such as
building up its military or developing new alliances—other states perceive these actions as
threats to their own security.
○ This perception often compels them to respond by strengthening their own
defences, leading to a cycle of suspicion, competition and escalation.
● The security dilemma demonstrates how defensive actions, intended to increase security,
can paradoxically reduce security for all states involved, increasing the likelihood of conflict.
● The arms race during the Cold War is a classic example of the security dilemma in action.
○ As the United States and the Soviet Union each sought to outpace the other in
nuclear weapons development, their defensive measures were interpreted as
aggressive, escalating tensions and fostering a climate of mutual distrust.
The Inevitability of War
Realists believe that war is an inevitable feature of the anarchic international system. They argue
that the absence of a global authority to enforce rules or mediate disputes creates a world where
conflicts over power, resources, and security are bound to arise. In this view, war is not abnormal,
but instead an inevtiable outcome of the competitive dynamics of global politics.
● Realists believe that states, as self-interested actors, are driven by the pursuit of power and
the need to ensure their survival. This quest for power often brings states into conflict with
one another, as the gains of one state can threaten the security of another.
○ In an anarchic system, this zero-sum nature of power struggles means that states
must always be prepared to defend their interests, even through the use of force.
● Realists also emphasise the limits of international institutions and treaties in preventing war.
Without a global authority to enforce agreements, states simply abandon treaties and act
unilaterally when it suits their interests.
○ For example, the League of Nations failed to prevent WWII, as great powers
including Germany, Japan, Great Britan and France sought to maximise their power
and came into conflict as a result.
