The Main Ideas Of Liberalism
Liberalism is a theory in international relations that emphasises the potential for cooperation,
progress, and the creation of a more peaceful global order. It offers a stark contrast to realism,
focusing on the capacity of states to work together for mutual benefit. Rooted in an optimistic view
of human nature, liberalism sees individuals, and therefore states, as capable of reason, morality,
and cooperation to reach shared goals.
Unlike realism, liberalism does not view the state as the sole actor in global politics. Instead they
believe that states are one of many actors that shape global affairs, including international
organisations, non-governmental organisations (NGOs) and multinational corporations, and even
individuals in shaping global affairs. These non-state actors create global norms, enforce
international law, and foster cooperation between states.
The philosophical foundation of liberalism is closely tied to John Locke, who argued that humans
are inherently rational and capable of coexisting in a natural society governed by laws and mutual
agreements, without an overarching sovereign. In the realm of international relations, this optimism
translates into a belief that states can transcend conflict through diplomacy, trade, and the
establishment of international norms and institutions.
The Significance of Morality and Optimism on Human Nature
Central to liberalism is a belief in the moral capacity of individuals and states. Liberals argue that
human beings (and therefore states) are not solely driven by self-interest or the pursuit of power,
as realists suggest, but are also motivated by justice, upholding values and working together for
mutual benefit. This optimism about human nature underpins the liberal view that international
relations are not doomed to perpetual conflict as realists aregued
● Liberal theorists maintain that through reason and dialogue, states can resolve disputes
and foster cooperation.
○ Rather than simply ignoring international law/agreements as realists suggest,
liberals argue that states will generally adhere to international law and norms, even
in the absence of an overarching global authority to enforce them.
○ Liberals argue that states recognise the long-term benefits of a rules-based
international order and the stability it provides.
● The belief in the potential for moral progress also drives liberal support for democracy and
human rights. Liberals believe that these values can be promoted by the international
community to create a more peaceful and cooperative world.
The Possibility of Harmony and Balance
Liberalism is grounded in the belief that harmony and balance can be achieved in global politics
through cooperation and mutual benefit. Unlike realism, which emphasises conflict as inevitable,
liberalism sees states and other actors as capable of working together to create a stable and
peaceful international system.
● One of the central beliefs of liberalism is that states are not always in zero-sum competition.
Instead, they can pursue positive-sum outcomes, where cooperation leads to benefits for
all parties involved.
